Announce what a reset clears, instead of assigning it silently

vterm_state_reset() clears mode.alt_screen and mouse_flags by direct
assignment, bypassing vterm_state_set_termprop() -- so nothing above
libvterm hears about either, and the screen layer is not told at all.

Two consequences, both reachable from bytes a remote sends (RIS, or
DECSTR):

  - VTERM_PROP_ALTSCREEN never fires, so screen->buffer keeps pointing at
    the alternate buffer while state->mode.alt_screen says primary. The
    terminal goes on drawing into a buffer it believes it has left.

  - VTERM_PROP_MOUSE never fires, so an application mirroring the mouse
    mode -- which it must, to decide whether a click is a report or a
    local selection -- keeps a mode libvterm no longer has. Every click
    is then routed to a remote that will not receive it.

Both are fixed by announcing rather than assigning. set_termprop already
does the whole job for these two properties, including the buffer swap
and the lineinfo switch, so the reset gets strictly more correct as well
as louder. Announced only when the mode was actually set, so a reset of
an already-primary, already-mouseless terminal stays silent.

The third part is a new property. A terminal built on libvterm has modes
libvterm does not model -- the kitty keyboard protocol's flags and
xterm's modifyOtherKeys are the live examples, and both specifications
say RIS clears them. There is currently no way to learn that a reset
happened, so those flags survive for the life of the terminal: after a
program dies without popping its kitty flags, Ctrl+C encodes as
CSI 99;5u for ever, no SIGINT is delivered, and the escape sequence
lands on the shell's command line instead.

VTERM_PROP_RESET is announced last, so a listener sees a terminal that is
already reset. It is announcement-only and stores nothing; it is listed
explicitly in set_termprop's switch so that adding it cannot silently
make that switch non-exhaustive.

Applied on top of 0001-0004 at build time (see the top-level Makefile);
upstream is otherwise untouched. Worth sending upstream -- the first two
parts are a plain bug fix, and the third is the smallest hook that makes
the first two useful to a terminal with modes of its own.

--- a/include/vterm.h
+++ b/include/vterm.h
@@ -260,6 +260,7 @@
   VTERM_PROP_CURSORSHAPE,       // number
   VTERM_PROP_MOUSE,             // number
   VTERM_PROP_FOCUSREPORT,       // bool
+  VTERM_PROP_RESET,             // bool; announced, never stored
 
   VTERM_N_PROPS
 } VTermProp;
--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -2141,13 +2141,26 @@
   state->mode.autowrap        = 1;
   state->mode.insert          = 0;
   state->mode.newline         = 0;
-  state->mode.alt_screen      = 0;
   state->mode.origin          = 0;
   state->mode.leftrightmargin = 0;
   state->mode.bracketpaste    = 0;
   state->mode.report_focus    = 0;
 
-  state->mouse_flags = 0;
+  /* Announced rather than assigned, so that a caller which mirrors these
+   * hears about them. Both were cleared silently: the screen layer was
+   * left pointing at the alternate buffer while the state said primary,
+   * and an application tracking VTERM_PROP_MOUSE kept a mouse mode the
+   * terminal no longer had.
+   */
+  if(state->mode.alt_screen)
+    settermprop_bool(state, VTERM_PROP_ALTSCREEN, 0);
+  else
+    state->mode.alt_screen = 0;
+
+  if(state->mouse_flags)
+    settermprop_int(state, VTERM_PROP_MOUSE, VTERM_PROP_MOUSE_NONE);
+  else
+    state->mouse_flags = 0;
 
   state->vt->mode.ctrl8bit   = 0;
 
@@ -2194,6 +2207,17 @@
     VTermRect rect = { 0, state->rows, 0, state->cols };
     erase(state, rect, 0);
   }
+
+  /* Last, so a listener sees a terminal that is already reset.
+   *
+   * The modes above are the ones libvterm models; a terminal built on top
+   * of it has its own -- the kitty keyboard flags and xterm's
+   * modifyOtherKeys are the live examples -- and both specifications say
+   * RIS clears them. Without this there is no way to know a reset
+   * happened, so those stay set for the life of the terminal and every
+   * keystroke is encoded for a protocol the application never enabled.
+   */
+  settermprop_bool(state, VTERM_PROP_RESET, 1);
 }
 
 void vterm_state_get_cursorpos(const VTermState *state, VTermPos *cursorpos)
@@ -2293,6 +2317,11 @@
   case VTERM_PROP_FOCUSREPORT:
     state->mode.report_focus = val->boolean;
     return 1;
+  case VTERM_PROP_RESET:
+    /* Nothing to store: the announcement IS the value. Listed rather
+     * than left to fall through so that adding it cannot silently make
+     * this switch non-exhaustive. */
+    return 1;
 
   case VTERM_N_PROPS:
     return 0;
